17592186045417] [17592186326529 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186326186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326529 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326529 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326529 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186326186 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326529 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aa5eacfc-70b8-5d6e-b1f9-f5af4734a6b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468567 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468568 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186468569 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1069 "q-50971" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following series converge?\n\nI. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}$\n\nII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}$\n\nIII. ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a500c68-ffb3-5162-a19f-22fd0f314af1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186332840 #edb/ref 1074 "We use the flowchart below to determine which tests to use for each series.\n\n![](images/3a/3aa7f0243efeab51d00aa9694dd018245f7ca4664cc1a5fdf4e646aebb61536e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's test each series in turn.\n\n- For the first series, we use the alternating series test. Observe that \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{n!}\n$$\n\nis positive and decreasing for all $n\\ge 1,$ and ${a}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ So, by the alternating series test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n!}\n$$\n\nis also convergent.\n\n- For the second series, we use the limit comparison test. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\,{b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nWorking out the necessary limit, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{b}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}÷\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\\cdot \\sqrt{n}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2n}{n+2}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{2}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{1+0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $L=2$ is positive and finite. Now, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nis divergent by the $p$-series test. So, the series \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2\\sqrt{n}}{n+2}\n$$\n\nis also divergent by the limit comparison test.\n\n- For the final series, we use the $n$th term test for divergence. Let \n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we have ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\infty ,$ since the polynomial in the numerator grows faster than the polynomial in the denominator. Therefore, by the $n$th term test, the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{n}^{3}}{n+2{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nis divergent.\n\nIn conclusion, only series I converges." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336355 #edb/ref 1080 "45\\,\\text{customers}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336355 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336355 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2293784d-4698-5b95-b839-b9abb50be6eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336561 #edb/ref 1080 "55\\,\\text{customers}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336561 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336561 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "38cb20fb-9218-58ed-adce-b046db171551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336917 #edb/ref 1080 "53\\,\\text{customers}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336917 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186336917 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "628eec49-1d46-5ac1-8a64-39bd0c3c41de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337492 #edb/ref 1080 "62\\,\\text{customers}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186337492 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 1759